264 de'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang , mi'i las kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas de'i cha shas gcig gi rigs 'dra phyi ma chur skye ba dang Another part of the glass of water again provides a material cause, and the karma of the human provides a contributing factor; and then based on both of these the later continuation of one part of the glass of water starts being water.
265 yang de'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang , lha'i las kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, de'i cha shas gcig gi rigs 'dra phyi ma bdud rtsir skye ba sogs yin pa'i phyir Yet another part of the glass of water provides a material cause, and the karma of the pleasure being provides a contributing factor; and then based on both of these the later continuation of one part of the glass of water starts being ambrosia, and so on.
266 de'i tshe na brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du yod kyang , gsum gas gsum ga mthong ba ma yin te, yi dvags kyi las ngan pa bsags pa'i dbang gis rnag khrag tu mthong , dngos po gzhan gnyis ma mthong ba dang , de bzhin du 'gro ba 'og ma gnyis la yang shes par bya ba yin pa'i phyir At this point, the glass full of wet and flowing water is something with three different parts. Nonetheless, it is not the case that all three different beings see all three parts. The craving spirit is forced by the bad karma he has collected to see the glass of water as pus and blood; and he doesn't see the other two things. One should understand that a similar case holds with the latter two types of beings.
267 de ltar 'byung ba de yang brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de blta bya thun mong ba yin pa'i dbang du byas pa yin gyi, yi dvags kyis lag tu blangs nas longs spyod par brtsams pa de'i tshe na khyad chos gsum ldan du skye ba ma yin te, de'i tshe na yi dvags kyi thun mong ma yin pa'i longs spyod bya yin pas, rigs 'dra phyi ma rnag khrag tu skye ba yin pa'i phyir What we just described as happening is only with reference to where a glass of something wet and flowing is an object shared by the three different beings, as they look at it together. When the craving spirit himself though picks up the glass in his hand and begins to partake of its contents, the glass of liquid is no longer something that exists with three different parts. Since at this point it is something that the craving spirit is experiencing exclusively, its continuation starts being pus and blood.
268 dang por gang du gnas pa de yang 'gro ba gsum po de'i rang rang gi thun mong ma yin pa'i las la brten nas grub pa'i snod kyi 'jig rten gang nas blangs pa de nyid du grub pa yin te, mi'i thun mong ma yin pa'i las la brten nas grub pas mi'i 'jig rten gyi chu dvangs shing bsil ba nas blangs na de nyid du gnas pa sogs yin pa'i phyir How the glass of liquid exists originally all depends on the particular outer world from where it has been taken, for each of the three different beings has a different outer world, depending on the specific karma he himself has collected. If the glass of liquid were sweet, cool water taken from the world of humans--a world created by the specific karma of the human in the group--then that would be its original condition, and so on.
269 yi dvags kyis chu klung la bltas pa na rnag khrag mthong ba de yang yi dvags 'ga' zhig la yin gyi, yi dvags thams cad la ma yin te, yi dvags la phyi'i sgrib pa can, nang gi sgrib pa can, zas skom nyid la yod pa'i sgrib pa can du ma yod pa'i phyir When we say that a craving spirit looks at a stream of water and sees pus and blood, by the way, we are only talking about some kinds of craving spirits, and not all of them. This is because there are many kinds of craving spirits: some with obstacles in the world around them that prevent them from relieving their craving; some with obstacles that are parts of their bodies; and some with obstacles that relate to the food or drink itself.
270 brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad mas grub pa gsum skye ba'i dpe yang yod de, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang lcags sngags kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na tsha ba'i reg bya mi myong reg bya gzhan zhig myong ba dang , lcags sngags ma btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na, tsha ba'i reg bya myong reg bya gzhan mi myong ba dang There is, moreover, an example we can use for how, when the three different types of beings with their three karmas look all together at a glass full of something wet and flowing, there start to be three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception. Suppose there is a ball of red-hot steel; one piece of this ball provides the material cause, and the "mantra of steel" provides a contributing factor. Due to these two, a person who has used the mantra of steel on his hand can touch the ball, but he doesn't undergo any sensation of heat; instead, he feels some other sensation. A person who has not used the mantra on his hand touches the ball and does feel a sensation of heat, and no other kind of sensation.
271 sos ka'i dus su zla ba'i reg bya'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang , yi dvags kyi las kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, de'i lus shes kyi myong byar gyur pa'i tsha ba'i reg bya skye ba dang , dgun dus su nyi ma'i reg bya'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang , yi dvags kyi las kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, de'i lus shes kyi myong byar gyur pa'i grang ba'i reg bya skye ba yod pa lta bu yin pa'i phyir Another example would be the moon in springtime; one part of the feel of its rays on the body provides the material cause, and then the karma of a craving spirit provides a contributing factor. Based on these two, the spirit gets a sensation of heat, which is experienced by the consciousness of the body. So too with the wintertime sun; one part of the feel of its rays on the body provides the material cause, and then the karma of the craving spirit provides a contributing factor. Based on these two, the spirit gets a sensation of cold, which is experienced by the consciousness of the body.
272 de ltar skye ba yin te, bshes spring las, yi dvags rnams la sos ka'i dus su ni, , zla ba'ang tsha la dgun ni nyi ma'ang grang , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir It is a fact that they get this kind of sensation, for [Arya Nagarjuna's] Letter to a Friend states: For craving spirits, even the light of the moon In the spring is hot, and even the winter sun cold.
273 de yang yul dus khyad par can de dag gi dbang gis skye ba yin gyi spyir de ltar skye ba ma yin te, nyi ma'i steng du grang ba'i reg bya yang med, zla ba'i steng du tsha ba'i reg bya yang med pa'i phyir, der thal, nyi ma'i steng du mun pa'i rdzas med pa'i phyir All of this is caused by the extraordinary circumstances of the particular time and place, for generally speaking it never happens this way: there is nothing at all about the sun that can feel cold, and nothing about the moon that can feel hot. This too is a fact, for there does not exist on the sun any case of that substance we call "covered space."
274 gsum pa de la rtsod pa spong ba la, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po la, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, cha shas gsum ldan gyi cha can du skyes kyang , 'gro ba gsum po des rang rang gi thun mong ma yin pa'i las kyi dbang gis, 'gro ba re res dngos po gsum ga ma mthong bar khas blangs pa la Here is the third part of our presentation, where we refute the rebuttal of representatives of other views. You will recall that our own position is describing a situation where beings of three different types, each with their own karma, are sitting together and looking at a glass filled with something that is wet and flowing. The glass filled with something wet and flowing exists, at this point, as something with three distinct parts. Nonetheless, no one of the beings is able to see all three things there, for they are each at the mercy of the particular karma that they themselves have collected. Representatives of other viewpoints now come to attack this position.
275 kha cig, de ltar 'dod pa de mi 'thad par thal, dpal ldan chos kyi grags pa'i lung dang 'gal ba'i phyir, der thal, de'i gzhung las, gal te de yang mthong med la, , brten nas gzugs gzhan 'byung 'gyur na, , zhes dgra gnyen gnyis kyis skyes bu gcig gi gzugs la bltas pa de'i tshe na, skyes bu de'i gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis su gnas kyang , gzugs de dang dgra gnyen gnyis kyi bar du las gzugs skyes pas, dgra gnyen gnyis po des gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis ka ma mthong ba, grangs can gyis khas blangs pa de 'gog byed kyi rigs pa kha spos pas khyod kyi de la gnod pa'i phyir zer na One comes and makes the following claim: Your position, as just explained, is mistaken, for it goes against a statement of the glorious Dharmakirti. This is quite surely the case, for in his major work [entitled The Commentary on Valid Perception] he says, Suppose you say that they don't see it, And circumstances cause another form. What he's talking about here is a belief of the [non- Buddhist] Numerist School. They give the case of a single person whose physical form is looked upon at the same time by his enemy, and also by his friend. In reality, the person's physical form is both attractive and ugly at the same time. Something happens where yet another physical form, one from karma, grows up between the person's true physical form and the enemy and friend looking at it. Because of this neither the enemy nor the friend sees both the attractiveness and the ugliness together. Master Dharmakirti uses logic to refute this concept, and this same logic can be used against the position you have taken, to prove that you are wrong.
276 de mi 'thad par thal, de ltar 'dod pa de ni chos kyi grags pa'i lung don dang , phyogs snga smra ba po'i 'dod pa gnyis ka khong du ma chud pa'i skyon yin pa'i phyir Your reasoning here is though incorrect, for the belief you have expressed shows that you have failed to understand both the meaning of Master Dharmakirti's statement, and the whole position expressed above.
277 der thal, chos kyi grags pa'i lung don ni, grangs can gyis rgyang ring thung gi gzugs la bltas pa de'i tshe na, rgyang ring thung gi gzugs dang , mig shes gnyis kyi bar du, las gzugs gsal mi gsal skye ba'i dbang gis, gzugs gsal bar snang mi snang 'jog pa yin gyi, gzugs kyi rnam pa shar ba'i sgo nas gzugs gsal bar snang mi snang 'jog pa ma yin zhes khas blangs pa la, de lta bu'i las gzugs gnyis po des rgyang ring thung gi gzugs gnyis po de bsgribs sam ma bsgribs, bsgribs na mig shes kyis gzugs gnyis po de ma mthong bar thal, des de sgrib pa'i phyir This is a fact, for the actual meaning of Master Dharmakirti's statement is as follows. The Numerist School is describing a situation where you are looking at a physical form either from far away, or from up close. They say that, depending on the distance between you and the object, another physical form which is the result of karma, and which stands between your visual consciousness and the original form, is either clear or not. This then determines whether the original form appears to you distinctly or not. It is not the case though, they say, that what determines whether the original form appears clearly or not is whether or not you have a clear impression of this form. In reply then Master Dharmakirti is asking the Numerists: Let's consider these two cases of some intermediate physical form that comes from karma. Do they, or do they not, function to obscure the two original forms, the one at a distance, and the other close by? If they were to obscure them, then your visual consciousness could never see the two original forms, since they would have been obscured by the others.
278 ma bsgribs na, mig shes des las gzugs gnyis dang rgyang ring thung gi gzugs gnyis po de cig car du mthong bar thal, des de ma bsgribs pa'i phyir zhes bkag pa'i don yin pa'i phyir And suppose you say that they do not obscure them. Wouldn't your visual consciousness then see both the two intermediate forms created by karma, and the two original forms, the near one and the far one, all at the same time? They would have to because, according to you, the intermediate forms do not obscure the original ones. This is the real point of the Master's statement, wherein he refutes that belief of the Numerists.
279 gnyis pa der thal, phyogs snga mas brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la, 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun du yang khas ma blangs, skyes bu gcig gi gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis ka yin par yang khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyis mig shes dang chu klung gi bar du rnag khrag gi las gzugs skye bar khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyi mig gis rnag khrag gi las gzugs dang chu klung gnyis ka mthong bar yang khas ma blangs, las kyi dbang gis yi dvags kyi chu klung ma mthong bar khas blangs pa'i phyir Our second point [that you have failed to comprehend the position we expressed above] is also quite true. Our original position was describing a situation where three different kinds of beings were sitting together and looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It is not our position that the glass full of something wet and flowing is one thing that is three different things. And it is not our position that there is such a thing as the physical appearance of a person which is at once both attractive and ugly. It is furthermore not our position that the blood and pus represent some kind of physical form which results from karma and grows up between the visual consciousness of the craving spirit and the stream of water. And it is not our position that the craving spirit's eyes see both this blood and pus as some kind of physical form resulting from karma, and the river of water at the same time. It is our position that, by force of his karma, the craving spirit is not able to see the stream of water.
ARCHIVES [{"local": "/Users/adamderickandrade/Documents/ChatGPT/Geshe Michael Roach Tib _ Eng Alignment/campaign-worktree/data/hgm_dictionary_v27_2.json.gz", "archive": "/Users/adamderickandrade/Desktop/HGM DICTIONARY - TRANSLATION APP/Handoff/release/hgm_dictionary_v27_2.json.gz", "local_sha256": "3a99603cd9428f0d04e67c7d2790cc093f272162e7bf6278906a4b635f962253", "archive_sha256": "3a99603cd9428f0d04e67c7d2790cc093f272162e7bf6278906a4b635f962253", "bytes_identical": true}, {"local": "/Users/adamderickandrade/Documents/ChatGPT/Geshe Michael Roach Tib _ Eng Alignment/campaign-worktree/data/full_parallel_corpus_v32.json.gz", "archive": "/Users/adamderickandrade/Desktop/HGM DICTIONARY - TRANSLATION APP/Handoff/data/full_parallel_corpus_v32.json.gz", "local_sha256": "1a1d8764a960c119d227c3cb7a7b0cb03073f9dc54116a3ec4e585c40323ddf8", "archive_sha256": "1a1d8764a960c119d227c3cb7a7b0cb03073f9dc54116a3ec4e585c40323ddf8", "bytes_identical": true}]
REGISTER {"query": "All register records: exact C05 seqs 286-288 and registered classes E-044/E-071/E-076/E-107/E-111/E-121 or heading glue", "register_sha256": "dc851e07fe913a904dc8b57f173f0bcbc4f13a884e65136f6560688576dc6c6c", "type": "list", "top_keys": [], "total": 192, "matches": [{"item_id": "E-044", "course": "C01", "segment": "C01:6", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "acip: ,AI Dm GU RU RA{tn} MAnd LA Km NI{ry}'A TA YA MI, ,   |   wylie: ai dM gu ru ra maND la kM ni'a ta ya mi,", "expected": "The Wylie should carry the braced Sanskrit stacks the ACIP encodes: \"ratna\" (from RA{tn}) and \"nirya\" (from NI{ry}). Cf. the same mantra at TCS07:82, which is intact: \"ai daM gu ru ratna maNDa la kaM niryA ta yA mi\".", "evidence": "The seg_hint of 40 is WRONG: C01:40 is the Pabongka title colophon (\"rdo rje 'chang pha bong kha pa...\"). The described strings (dM/kM/ND capitals, ni'a ta ya mi, ra maND la kM) occur at C01:6, verified verbatim. The ACIP source is CORRECT \u2014 {tn} and {ry} are the ACIP escapes for Sanskrit stacks, as at C01:344 (BU{ddh} P'A LI TA = Buddhapalita). The derived Wylie column silently drops the braced content: C01:6 \"ra maND la\" loses tn, \"ni'a\" loses ry. The parallel English in the same segment prints the correct \"Idam guru ratna mandalakam niryatayami\" twice, and TCS07:82 carries the undamaged Wylie.", "severity": "MEDIUM", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "Systematic, not local: the same converter loss produces \"bu pA li ta\" for Buddhapalita at C01:344 (HIGH severity there \u2014 a reader is taught a wrong author name with no adjacent correction), and leaks raw braces at C03:351 (\"a{u2} Ta'i\" from Sm {bh}A{u2} tA'I). Scored MEDIUM here only because C01:6's own English line supplies the correct reading inches away. The fix belongs in the ACIP-to-Wylie conversion, not in the source text. The trailing \"COVERAGE GATE: C01 474->477; 19 open\" in the note is campaign bookkeeping and carries no erratum."}, {"item_id": "E-046", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:6", "kind": "NOT_AN_ERRATUM", "found": "acip: ,AI Dm GU RU RA{tn} MAnd LA Km NI{ry}'A TA YA MI, ,   |   wylie: ai dM gu ru ra maND la kM ni'a ta ya mi,", "expected": "The Wylie should carry the braced Sanskrit stacks the ACIP encodes: \"ratna\" (from RA{tn}) and \"nirya\" (from NI{ry}). Cf. TCS07:82, intact: \"ai daM gu ru ratna maNDa la kaM niryA ta yA mi\".", "evidence": "seg_hint 6 is CORRECT and verifies verbatim. This is the same mandala-offering segment as E-044, repeated identically at seq 6 in all eighteen courses C01-C18 (verified: 18 identical rows). The ACIP is correct; the derived Wylie drops the {tn} and {ry} stacks. TCS07:82 and TCS07:334/406/454 carry the undamaged Wylie of the same mantra.", "severity": "MEDIUM", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "DUPLICATE OF E-044 in substance \u2014 same defect, same shared segment, different course. The editor should treat E-044/E-046 as one converter fix that will clear all 18 courses at once; do not open eighteen tickets. The note's closing remark \"Verse capitalization flattened, kept verbatim\" describes a deliberate campaign handling decision and is not an erratum. [reclassified: self-declared duplicate, kept as a record so a later pass does not re-raise it]"}, {"item_id": "E-071", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:303", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "the preliminaries", "expected": "", "evidence": "Verified in build/hgm_spine_v27_2.db, row id=867. C03:303 stores wylie='the preliminaries' (17 chars, confirmed with length(wylie)) and english='I. Why the Steps?' (17 chars); the acip column holds 'THE PRELIMINARIES' \u2014 English sitting in the transliteration column \u2014 and raw carries the same. The wylie is not Wylie: 'preliminaries' has no legal syllable analysis ('prel' is not a possible stack, 'aries' has no root-letter reading). Three courses carry the IDENTICAL wylie against unrelated English, which proves the string is a repeated editorial running head rather than any segment's text: C01:43 'the preliminaries' / 'I. The Lama and the Word'; C09:171 'the preliminaries' / 'The preliminary division of my text has two parts of its own: the offering of praise, and an identification of that which is to be to be explained.'; C03:303 / 'I. Why the Steps?'. The mechanism is visible where the same header stream was concatenated in front of real Tibetan instead of being stranded alone: C03:298 wylie = \"how the teaching was given ,de la 'dir khyab bdag 'khor lo'i mgon po rdo rje 'chang...\" and C09:54 wylie = \"i. the preliminaries de la 'dir rje btsun chos kyi rgyal po shar tsong kha pa chen po blo...\". Sibling header rows of the same class in this course: C03:22 'the asian classics institute'; C03:294 'foreword'; C03:295 'about the authors'; C03:401 'steps shared with those of lesser capacity'; C03:430 'steps shared with those of medium capacity'; C03:476 'open steps for those of greater capacity'; C03:513 'secret steps for those of greater capacity'; C03:558 'the conclusion'. Class boundary, to prevent over-broad remediation: C03:263 wylie='the tshom' / english='Destructive Doubts vi.' IS genuine Wylie (the tshom = doubt) and is NOT part of this class. Effect: the segment carries no Tibetan, so it cannot be aligned, and wylie-side FTS or concordance queries return English strings as though they were Tibetan. No textual correction is possible \u2014 the remedy is reclassification as an editorial section header rather than a Tibetan-English parallel segment.", "severity": "MEDIUM", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-076", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:296", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "You Should Give Up.1", "expected": "You Should Give Up.  [footnote 1]", "evidence": "CLASS ENTRY, not a single instance. Printed footnote reference numbers have been flattened into the running English at ingest, fused to the sentence-final period with no separator. Measured across the whole spine: 307 occurrences in 281 segments across 5 courses - C01 161, C03 95, C16 38, TCS13 7, TCS15 6. Counted on the signature 'sentence-final punctuation + 1-3 digits + space + capital', which excludes thousands separators ('25,000'), section labels ('(S1)') and verse citations ('[I.1b]'); two looser patterns were tried first and gave 1,054 and 867 before those contaminants were excluded. Every instance breaks exact-match search on the final word of its sentence.", "severity": "MEDIUM", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-106", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:429", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "our Lama to help us find the ability to do so. Notes to Reading Nine 37 Three lower realms: According to Buddhism there are six different types of rebirth.", "expected": "our Lama to help us find the ability to do so.", "evidence": "Measured on the spine row: the english field is 1500 characters; the translation of the Tibetan (which ends `gsol ba btab pa yin gsungs`) ends at character 499 with \"do so.\" (character 499) The remaining 1001 characters are the Reading Nine endnote apparatus \u2014 the heading \"Notes to Reading Nine\" plus the full BODIES of notes 37 and 38 and the opening of note 39 \u2014 and the field ends mid-sentence, verbatim \"there are five \" (trailing space, no terminator). This exceeds the registered heading-only class (\"a following section's heading glued onto the end\"): the parallel witness P1:77 carries the identical Tibetan and the identical 499-character translation and shows exactly the registered form (translation, then only the next heading \"Asian Classics Institute Reading Three: \u2026 V. Learning How to Want Freedom\"), whereas C03:429 has absorbed a section body. The following heading is separately segmented here as C03:430 (\"V. Learning How to Want Freedom\"); a spine query for \"Three lower realms: According to Buddhism\" returns C03:429 and nothing else, so the Reading Nine notes exist in the corpus only as this buried, truncated tail. Alignment impact: the tail inflates English-side counts for this segment (e.g. it is where the ambiguity of common words must be measured) and is unsearchable as notes. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-01 (independent re-check, survived HIGH/CONFIRMED, two corrections): (1) P1:77's Tibetan is NOT identical \u2014 it differs by three punctuation marks (comma/shad placement); the 499-character translation is identical. (2) THE MID-SENTENCE CUT IS OURS. The english field is exactly 1500 characters because the spine ingest caps it: engines/hgm_tools.py, ' '.join(eng)[:1500]. The glued endnote bodies are a source-layout defect; the truncation at 'there are five ' is a pipeline defect. Siblings C03:376 and C03:620 (the Reading Eight and Ten note blocks) are also glued and also exactly 1500 characters; see the class entry filed alongside for the corpus-wide count. Field tail verbatim: 'there are five ' (trailing space, 1500th character).", "severity": "HIGH", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-107", "course": "corpus-wide", "segment": "class entry (186 segments end at exactly 1,500 characters)", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "' '.join(eng)[:1500] in engines/hgm_tools.py - the spine ingest silently caps every segment's English at 1,500 characters", "expected": "the whole English of the segment; a segment whose translation runs past 1,500 characters loses its tail with no marker, no count and no log line", "evidence": "Found by the skeptic pass on C03:429 (E-106), which asked why that field ends mid-sentence at 'there are five ' and measured length(english) = 1500 exactly. Census 2026-09-01 on build/hgm_spine_v27_2.db: 186 segments have length(english) = 1500 (DDA 28, C18 15, C12 14, C13 12, C11 11, Sunlight 11, C17 10, C03 9 - including C03:376, 429, 620, the Reading Eight/Nine/Ten note blocks - MB2 8, C16 7, and 25 further courses); 34 segments EXCEED 1,500, so at least one ingest path does not apply the cap and its reach is not uniform. What is lost is not always apparatus: wherever the cap falls inside a translation, the corpus has no English for the rest of the Tibetan and nothing can align it. The 186 are a floor for the affected rows (a row cut at exactly the cap is certain; a row that happened to be shorter is unaffected).", "severity": "HIGH", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "OURS, NOT FIXED. The cap lives in the canonical ingest (engines/hgm_tools.py), so removing it changes the spine itself and must be re-proved against the full batteries (inviolable rule 2) before any layer is rebuilt on it; the C03:376/429/620 note blocks and any truncated translation would need re-scanning afterwards. Opened in TODO.md 2026-09-01 for Adam's ruling and the data project. No document edit is asked of ALL for this entry - it is a pipeline defect on our side."}, {"item_id": "E-111", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:440", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "phyi ma'i snang shas", "expected": "the row's Tibetan should close the verse line: \"...YANG YANG BSAMS PAS PHYI MA'I SNANG SHAS LDOG,\"", "evidence": "QUOTED AS FILED BY THE RECONCILER: wylie/ACIP of C03:440 end at \"YANG YANG BSAMS PAS PHYI MA'I SNANG SHAS\" (no closing shad) while the row's English ends \"...And the cycle's suffering: stop desire for the future.\" || Confirmed from this row's own text. (1) The quoted verse is in nine-syllable metre: line 1 of the citation, las 'bras mi slu 'khor ba'i sdug bsngal rnams, has nine syllables; line 2, yang yang bsams pas phyi ma'i snang shas, has eight, so the row ends one syllable short of the line. (2) Every other verse line in the row closes with a shad (\", ,\"); the row ends at snang shas with none. (3) The row's English renders the complete line including its verb (\"stop desire for the future\"), and \"stop\" has no Tibetan anywhere in the row. The missing syllable is ldog: C03:441's wylie begins \"ldog,ces pa ltar thog mar 'khor ba'i phun tshogs...\" while its English begins \"Suppose at first we are able to find...\", so the 440/441 boundary is offset by one word. The undivided line is witnessed at C01:207 and C16:98 (\"...PHYI MA'I SNANG SHAS LDOG,CES PAS BSTAN TE\") and at P1:83 (\"...phyi ma'i snang shas ldog\", same source re-ingested, corroborative only). The same split recurs two rows later: C03:443 ends \"...yid la goms pas tshe 'di'i snang shas\" with English \"...Think it constantly, stop desire for this life.\" and C03:444 opens \"ldogs pa ltar...\". Effect: \"stop\" in this row is necessarily left unwrapped, and ldog in C03:441 has no English on its row. Nothing is lost across the pair, hence LOW. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-01 (two independent skeptics, one on each side of the boundary, both survived): C03:440's wylie, ACIP and raw ingest record stop at 'snang shas' and 'ldog,ces' opens 441; the independent witnesses C01:207 and C16:98 carry the undivided line '...phyi ma'i snang shas ldog,ces pas bstan te'. The mirror claim filed from 441 ('ldog,' as the tail of 440's verse) is the same defect and is folded into this record rather than billed twice. THE SAME CUT RECURS at eight C03 row boundaries: 36/37, 163/164, 165/166 (filed as E-062), 440/441, 443/444, 472/473, 594/595, 610/611 - a verse line's final syllable spilling into the next row is a class, not a one-off; recorded here as a list rather than as a class entry so every citation stays checkable.", "severity": "LOW", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-121", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:497", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "all six perfections77 are covered", "expected": "all six perfections are covered", "evidence": "Footnote marker 77 fused to the preceding word (not to a period, so outside the registered period-fusion class). Re-checked by the reconciler: parallel witness C07:81 carries the identical English sentence and identical wylie but reads 'all six perfections are covered' with no digits; the ACIP field of C03:497 has no numeral. Published reading confirmed; local C03 ingest artefact. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-03 (survived, LOW/CONFIRMED): the ACIP and wylie carry no numeral; the independent witness C07:81 has identical wylie and English minus the '77'; the register's footnote class E-076 covers period-fusion only, so this word-fused marker is a distinct, unregistered artefact.", "severity": "LOW", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-122", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:499", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "THOGBYE BRAG TU RANG RANG GIS SO THAR", "expected": "BYE BRAG TU RANG RANG GIS SO THAR (with THOG restored to the end of C03:498, '...LEGS PAR BSRUNG BA'I THOG')", "evidence": "This segment's ACIP (source of record) opens 'THOGBYE BRAG TU' and its wylie 'thogbye brag tu'; 'thogbye' is not a Tibetan syllable. Verified by re-query: C03:498 ends '...mi dge bcu spong gi tshul khrims legs par bsrung ba'i' (no thog). Independent parallel witness C07:82 ends '...mi dge bcu spong gi tshul khrims legs par bsrung ba'i thog' and C07:83 opens 'BYE BRAG TU RANG RANG GIS SO THAR, BYANG...' with English identical to this segment ('More specifically, with this first type of morality...'). P1:111 also reads 'bye brag tu rang rang gis...' (same text re-ingested; corroborating, not independent). Corpus-wide, 'thogbye' occurs only in C03:499 and 'thog bye brag tu' occurs nowhere, so this is a one-off segmentation fault at the C03 498/499 boundary: the stranded final syllable of 498 was glued to the head of 499. Not in any registered class. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-03 (survived; severity set LOW, consistent with E-111, since nothing is lost): the fused THOGBYE is verbatim in the ACIP and wylie while 498 ends at BA'I with no thog; the independent witness C07:82/83 reads '...bsrung ba'i thog | bye brag tu ...'; 'thogbye' is not a possible Tibetan syllable. The same row-boundary mechanism as E-111 but a distinct class: a PROSE token fused across the cut rather than a verse line's final syllable spilled. Not in E-111's list.", "severity": "LOW", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-131", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:592", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "gcig bsdus pa, lags kyang , ,rje thub pa'i dbang po'i gsung rab kun, ,don ma tshang med par gcig bsdus pa", "expected": "The block 'lags kyang , ,rje thub pa'i dbang po'i gsung rab kun, ,don ma tshang med par gcig bsdus pa' should appear once; the second copy is a dittograph. (The Tibetan of the English lines that seem orphaned here is carried whole in C03:593 \u2014 a translation-order row skew, not lost text.)", "evidence": "ACIP source of record carries the same repeat: '...GCIG BSDUS PA, LAGS KYANG , ,RJE THUB PA'I DBANG PO'I GSUNG RAB KUN, ,DON MA TSANG MED PAR GCIG BSDUS PA,'. P1:153 has the identical duplication but is the same text re-ingested (same lineage, not independent). SSL:311/312/371 match only on 'blo bzang grags pa' (the colophon of the Lam rim), not this verse. C03:593 supplies the Tibetan for the orphaned English lines. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-03: refuted=false \u2014 86-char block byte-identical twice in both wylie and ACIP (offsets 116/208); only P1:153 (same lineage) repeats it; the 'missing Tibetan' half is NOT a defect: C03:593 carries the lines whole, so this is a row skew, not E-111. File the dittograph only; severity LOW, CONFIRMED.", "severity": "LOW", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-135", "course": "C03", "segment": "C03:611", "kind": "TIBETAN_SPELLING", "found": "GANGS GI''A", "expected": "uncertain - a single a-chung (P1:165 reads gi'a'; the English 'Ganggiu' suggests gangs gi'u)", "evidence": "Field: acip (source of record) reads 'ZHOL KHANG SRAS GANGS GI''A SPRUL SKU'; wylie mirrors it as gi''a. A doubled a-chung is not a legal Tibetan syllable. Parallel witness P1:165 (same lineage, same colophon) reads gi'a' - a DIFFERENT illegal form, so there is no published reading to defend either; the two copies disagree with each other. No other corpus row contains 'gangs gi'. Not in any registered class. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-03: refuted=false \u2014 source keying error: GANGS GI''A verbatim in the ACIP source of record, mirrored in wylie; a doubled a-chung has no legal reading and no headword; P1:165 same-lineage and reads a different illegal form GI'A'; E-044 does not apply. LOW/PROBABLE.", "severity": "LOW", "confidence": "PROBABLE", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-147", "course": "C04", "segment": "C04:98", "kind": "ENGLISH_TYPO", "found": "The two phrases terms \"holy great compassion\"", "expected": "The two terms \"holy great compassion\" (or: The two phrases ...) \u2014 one of the two synonymous nouns is a leftover from an edit", "evidence": "C04:98 english column carries two adjacent synonymous head nouns, 'phrases terms'; the wylie has no lexical source for either (the sentence is GMR's editorial frame for skabs 'dir ... gcig tu bshad). Parallel-witness check: the only other hit for 'two phrases terms' is C16:575, whose English is this row byte-for-byte plus a fused following heading ('14 On the Different Kinds of Compassion') \u2014 the same file re-ingested, not an independent published reading. TCS09:339 'the two phrases, finishing off...' is unrelated text. Not in a registered errata class. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-04 (Fable 5.1): refuted=false \u2014 verbatim in C04:98 English, no Tibetan source for either noun; sole parallel C16:575 is the same file re-ingested (173/198 C04 wylie lines recur in C16); not a registered class.", "severity": "LOW", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}, {"item_id": "E-158", "course": "C05", "segment": "C05:65", "kind": "INGEST_ARTEFACT", "found": "Where does the word \"knowledge\" [Sanskrit: abhidharma] come from? A phenomenon [dharma] is that which possesses [dhr\ufffd] a nature. And knowledge brings to [abhi] you, or leads you to [abhi] that highest of all existing phenomena: nirvana. 7 About the Expression \"Treasure House\"", "expected": "... A phenomenon [dharma] is that which possesses [dh\u1e5b] a nature. ... (the bracketed Sanskrit root is dh\u1e5b, 'to hold', the classical etymology of dharma; the damaged byte is U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER standing where the IAST \u1e5b was)", "evidence": "English field of C05:65 contains exactly one U+FFFD (hex EFBFBD) inside the bracket '[dhr\ufffd]'. The parallel C16:703 (same file re-ingested, not independent) carries the identical byte, so the loss predates the spine ingest. Class is spine-wide: 73 corpus_segments rows carry U+FFFD (e.g. C04:28 'prama\ufffd\ufffda', C08:26, C13:477, TCS02:91); docs/errata_register.json has NO entry for the mojibake/replacement-character class (searched for 'FFFD', 'mojibake', 'diacritic', 'prama', the character itself) and no record for C04:28 or C16:703, so the class is not yet registered. The corrupted text is GMR's Sanskrit apparatus, not Tibetan and not translation; the Tibetan/English pairing of the row is unaffected. Recommend a class entry (like E-107) rather than 73 per-row records. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-05 (Fable 5.1): refuted=false \u2014 U+FFFD verbatim in the English; C16:703 same file re-ingested with the identical damaged byte; mojibake class absent from the register; dh\u1e5b the correct etymology; LOW/CONFIRMED stand.", "severity": "LOW", "confidence": "CONFIRMED", "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"}], "match_count": 13}
